      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Sertraline 1000 mg once daily is five times the maximum recommended adult dose of 200 mg/day. At that dose the patient could develop serotonin syndrome, seizures, or QT prolongation, and normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that. It may be a typo, for example 100 mg instead of 1000 mg.\n\nAlso, medication orders normally have to come from a licensed prescriber. If you're relaying this for a clinician, they should confirm the intended dose and enter or co-sign the order.\n\nIf the intended dose is within the usual range, I can enter it right away. For example, 50 mg by mouth once daily is a typical starting dose, and 100 mg once daily is also common. Please send me the confirmed dose. If 1000 mg really is intended, I'll need the prescriber to confirm it explicitly and give the clinical rationale."
      }
